        Languages &amp; runtimes
        <span>Design · Implementation · Tooling</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="discipline__body">
        <p>
          Design and implementation of programming languages: grammar, parsing, type
          systems, intermediate representations, code generation, formatters, and the
          developer tooling around them. The work is judged by whether the language is
          coherent at the edges — errors, imports, packages, and the first ten minutes.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Language design</li>
          <li>Parsers</li>
          <li>Type systems</li>
          <li>IR &amp; codegen</li>
          <li>LLVM</li>
          <li>Diagnostics</li>
          <li>Formatters</li>
          <li>CLI</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="discipline" data-reveal>
      <span class="discipline__index">02</span>
      <h2 class="discipline__title">
        Native systems
        <span>Windows · C++ · Rust</span>
      </h2>
      <div class="discipline__body">
        <p>
          Windows software in C++ and Rust where the binary is part of the promise: no
          unnecessary runtime, no installer ceremony, no hidden service. Direct APIs,
          controlled memory, and performance that is measured rather than asserted.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>C++20</li>
          <li>Rust</li>
          <li>Win32</li>
          <li>Direct3D 11</li>
          <li>ImGui</li>
          <li>Performance</li>
          <li>Packaging</li>
          <li>Systems design</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="discipline" data-reveal>
